All scalar identifiers and policy labels are unitless. Stored call measurements
retain the call's units. Identifiers do not contain private equation coefficients
or certify validity. Save local models and input data separately.

The optional \code{front_door} list stores the geographic label in character \code{region}.
Integer \code{nvel_region}, \code{forest}, and \code{district} store agency codes. Character
\code{preset} names automatically selected example products and is missing when products were
supplied.

Logical \code{model_assumed} and \code{products_assumed} record automatic choices.
Character \code{model_source} distinguishes \code{caller_model},
\code{caller_taper_map}, and \code{nvel_default}. Logical
\code{taper_map_ignored} records whether an explicit model took precedence.
All these fields are unitless.
}
\section{Status and missing values}{
\code{ok} means completion. \code{no_feasible_log} means a valid result with no logs under the
specifications. Other codes require review before totals are reported.

Failed
trees can retain partial logs, so a log row does not establish a complete
calculation. \code{\link{status_codes}} lists codes, meanings, and sources.

Unavailable physical or scaled quantities remain missing. Outside-bark missing
values are not zeros. Zero deductions mean no deduction at that stage.

Some failed-tree totals are zero because no logs were selected. Always read
the tree status before interpreting a zero total. \code{status = FALSE}
omits status columns and reports nonzero tree statuses in warnings.

\code{status = TRUE} keeps diagnoses and suppresses those warnings. Structural
input errors still stop the call. Use \code{na_action = 'propagate'} in
\code{\link{product_summary}} when an incomplete sum should remain missing.

}
